t this equipment into your  processes  and you don't want any surprises  so as by way of introduction let's let's  kind of take a step back  and look at cells that we're culturing  so  as we've trended towards cell therapy  and gene therapy applications  we're seeing use of and growth of more  sensitive cells so  many of you are growing immortalized  cell lines like hek293 cells for example  just for production of cells or from  products from cells  but also many of you may be growing stem  cells for  direct use in human therapeutic  applications  certainly we're seeing a lot of  advancement and a lot of expansion  in looking at growth of immune cells  and finally human primary cells if you  think about  autologous cell therapy where you're  taking cells from the patient  modifying them and then putting them  back in the patient to treat  a condition in that patient  so the thing about cultured cells of  course is they're constantly  sampling the milieu around them that  that  in which they are so they're looking at  temperature humidity  atmosphere gas concentration nutrients  and stimulants and repressors in the  growth medium  companions are they alone in that cell  culture or are they  touching other related cells that may  trigger different gene expression or  differentiation and they're responding  to those conditions  and since the co2 incubator is there to  mimic  the in vivo conditions or you know the  conditions in the body from where the  cells originated  that means that each parameter affects  cell growth  and as we've just established cells  respond to changing conditions  so the take home there is that variation  in your incubation conditions  can cause variation in your process  results  because as your cells respond to  different conditions that can affect  gene expression or protein expression  and so on so now i want to take a look  at each of the  parameters in the provided by the  incubator individually  what are their effects on cells and what  happens when  we see variation in the incubator and  then compare different technologies for  achieving those  parameters so i think with an incubator  the first thing you think of is  temperature so  let's begin with temperature  so incubator temperature is there to  mimic the body temperature  so the reason we culture cells  especially human-derived cells at 37  degrees is because  that's body temperature and we know that  cells are extremely sensitive to  temperature and if you think about  part of your immune response is to  develop a fever  and you know that you don't feel very  well when your  temperature your body temperature is  above normal 37 degrees celsius  we also know that cells are generally  okay for a few days at refrigeration  the lower temperatures are generally  okay and this  is also physiological when you wake up  first thing in the morning before you've  had that first cup of coffee  your body temperature is often lower  than 37 degrees it might be 36.5 degrees  celsius  but higher temperatures are detrimental  to cells  in fact cells cannot withstand more than  a few hours of 2 degrees above normal  and of course this is in culture not  outside the body where  um you know a fever is also detrimental  but  maybe not as detrimental as uh itself  kind of naked in culture  so with that in mind let's look at how  recovery after a door opening of  temperature can vary  depending on the design of the incubator  so what i'm showing here is a table  showing recovery to 37 degrees or 98  percent  of 37 degrees after a 30 second door  opening so the inner and outer door of  the incubator was open for 30 seconds  and this is a common test for function  functional performance of an incubator  so left to right the small blue bar  shows recovery in five minutes  after a 30 second door opening and this  is an incubator with  a circulating fan and a temperature  probe located in the temp in the  incubator chamber  going left to right the yellow bar shows  a 