imes we all love to see  sunlight coming into our workspace so  make sure that that if you do have  windows such that sun  is shining on the incubator um use some  window blinds just to um keep that  sunlight from shining on the incubator  and then finally um if uh if overheating  is a concern for your incubator  an incubator with a backup temperature  probe  helps prevent overheating in the event  of a failure  of that primary temperature sensor  okay so that's temperature now i'm going  to look at the  second most important parameter provided  by the co2 incubator  and that is gas concentration so i'm  going to start with a little bit of  biology because i think  sometimes we think about the incubator  as just a place to put  the cells and then we can get on with  the next step in our day  and i think it's important to take a  step back and understand the biology  behind  all of these parameters and and how they  affect the cells  so why do we provide carbon dioxide well  i think everybody knows that  the reason we provide carbon dioxide in  an incubator is  to balance the ph  and so this is actually mimicking what's  going on  in the bloodstream so in this case uh  the pink kind of cloud represents my  sort of cartoon of the lobe of a lung  and the arrow represents a blood vessel  so  so the purple cells are blood cells that  are deprived of oxygen they're bringing  waste carbon dioxide which is a waste  by-product  of every cell in the body and so  they're actually less red as they're  carrying carbon dioxide instead of  oxygen  and it turns out that the oxygen  concentration in the lungs  where it's you know kind of full of  waste carbon dioxide produced by the  cell  is five percent co2 or 40 millimeters of  mercury  pressure and of course that's the most  common  co2 setting on the incubator is five  percent  so the incubator is mimicking what's  going on in the body  we also have oxygen in the lungs of  course  oxygen in the earth's atmosphere the air  we breathe is between  20 and 21 percent but in the lungs  the oxygen concentration is much lower  it's 14 percent  and this is because oxygen doesn't  diffuse very well  so of course what's happening in the  bloodstream in the lungs is that  waste metabolism waste co2 from the  cells is being expelled  into the lungs and you exhale it in your  breath and oxygen is coming in  now the other thing that's happening in  the bloodstream along with the co2  is there are natural bicarbonate ions  which serve as a natural buffer in the  bloodstream  and the bicarbonate ion concentration  is 24 millimolar which is equal to two  milligrams per milliliter sodium  bicarbonate which is the buffer most  commonly used  in cell culture growth media and the  same concentration  in eagle's minimal essential medium upon  which  most growth media are based so as you  can see the conditions we're providing  in the incubator  are there to mimic exactly what's going  on in the body  now we can look at it from my  biochemistry level but  in short the growth media include that  buffer the sodium bicarbonate  to work with the co2 and balance the ph  and it turns out that unlike oxygen  carbon dioxide gas  dissolves and diffuses really easily in  liquid so there's an equilibrium  with the gas phase or that provided by  the incubator  of the carbon dioxide and the carbon  dioxide in the growth medium  and so the sodium bicarbonate is there  to  give off and take up ions or you know to  balance and buffer that ph  now it turns out that fluctuations in  the carbon dioxide can affect the cell  morphology and growth and  i think most of us working in cell  culture are aware of this  but my point here is not just about the  incubator but also  when you're looking at yourselves under  the microscope take an extra minute or  two to look at that cell morphology  are the cell membranes nice and tight  against the dish if you're working with  adherent cells  do you see any any um  vacuoles in the in the cytoplasm that  might be an early indication of toxicity  for example  and that might be something to look at  for your carbon dioxide  so now let's think about how does  how do we control carbon dioxide in the  incubator  so there are a couple of different  sensor technologies an earlier  technology  is called thermal conductivity and  in this type of sensor the gas is not  directly  measured but it comes as a function of  the relative humidity and the  temperature and that's in accordance  with  oil's gas law that the pressure of the  gas will change  with other gases in the system including  water vapor and also with temperature  many of us are using infrared sensors  these are great for  connecting with monitoring systems for  example  infrared sensors were first introduced  in incubators in 1984  and an infrared sensor is essentially  like  a like a light bulb like a older  incandescent light bulb it has a  tungsten filament  that when electrified gives off light  um and so the gas enters the sensor  it interacts with the light all  wavelengths  except 4.3 micrometers at which  co2 gas absorbs light are  filtered out and then and then that that  amount of co2 gas  triggers an influx of carbon dioxide gas  as needed  now the thing about a tungsten infrared  bulb is  that that tungsten filament degrades  over time as it's heated  and so as a because of that the light  intensity decreases over time as well  when the light intensity decreases that  contributes to decreased  accuracy of the measurement and drift  of that measurement so one of the  options  is to get a newer technology where  instead of using tungsten  a silicon light source is used to  provide that  light and this turns out to be much more  stable  and much more long-lived than tungsten  bulbs it's kind of the equivalent of  moving from an  incandescent light bulb to an led light  bulb  and so so as i said a silicon light  source is much more stable  and long-lived than an older tungsten  style infrared