d so many clean  rooms are set at 16 degrees celsius  and they're drier than the incubator or  even then a typical laboratory which is  about 50  humidity so that's that increases the  difference between  the ambient laboratory and the incubator  when you're in a clean room  also consider using larger culture  volumes and i assume that many of you  are  scaling up your culture vessels and so  of course that  also helps prevent evaporation just  because  you'll well any evaporation that does  occur will be less  dramatic because it'll be you know  dispersed  through a larger volume ensure proper  airflow make sure that your incubator  has a circulating fan  in the incubator chamber and then that  fan is properly cleaned and properly  operating  and then finally um if you're using  small volumes if you're using 96 well or  384 well plates these really small  volumes  are more susceptible to evaporation  and so one one option there is to use  this kind of unique  um the nunc edge multi-well plates where  there's a moat  surrounding the microwells that you can  fill with distilled water to  help kind of cushion that evaporation  and reduce evaporation from the  wells inside in the center of the plate  and it also reduces edge effect quite  quite a lot so that's  humidity we've also talked about  temperature  and carbon dioxide gas so  i just want to now kind of pull it all  together  and show you that all of these  parameters work together for cell  health so i know this is a little bit  hard to read but these are just um  just to remind you of each of those  graphs that i presented individually for  temperature  carbon dioxide and humidity but a lot of  times we don't talk about humidity for  example and we think more about  temperature than co2 but  i just wanted to emphasize that all of  these parameters work together for cell  health  and of course it's critical for cells to  spend maximal time at ideal conditions  because  as those conditions change that can  affec