imes we all love to see  sunlight coming into our workspace so  make sure that that if you do have  windows such that sun  is shining on the incubator um use some  window blinds just to um keep that  sunlight from shining on the incubator  and then finally um if uh if overheating  is a concern for your incubator  an incubator with a backup temperature  probe  helps prevent overheating in the event  of a failure  of that primary temperature sensor  okay so that's temperature now i'm going  to look at the  second most important parameter provided  by the co2 incubator  and that is gas concentration so i'm  going to start with a little bit of  biology because i think  sometimes we think about the incubator  as just a place to put  the cells and then we can get on with  the next step in our day  and i think it's important to take a  step back and understand the biology  behind  all of these parameters and and how they  affect the cells  so why do we provide carbon dioxide well  i think everybody knows that  the reason we provide carbon dioxide in  an incubator is  to balance the ph  and so this is actually mimicking what's  going on  in the bloodstream so in this case uh  the pink kind of cloud represents my  sort of cartoon of the lobe of a lung  and the arrow represents a blood vessel  so  so the purple cells are blood cells that  are deprived of oxygen they're bringing  waste carbon dioxide which is a waste  by-product  of every cell in the body and so  they're actually less red as they're  carrying carbon dioxide instead of  oxygen  and it turns out that the oxygen  concentration in the lungs  where it's you know kind of full of  waste carbon dioxide produced by the  cell  is five percent co2 or 40 millimeters of  mercury  pressure and of course that's the most  common  co2 setting on the incubator is five  percent  so the incubator is mimicking what's  going on in the body  we also have oxygen in the lungs of  course  oxygen in the earth's atmosphere the air  we br