plasm that  might be an early indication of toxicity  for example  and that might be something to look at  for your carbon dioxide  so now let's think about how does  how do we control carbon dioxide in the  incubator  so there are a couple of different  sensor technologies an earlier  technology  is called thermal conductivity and  in this type of sensor the gas is not  directly  measured but it comes as a function of  the relative humidity and the  temperature and that's in accordance  with  oil's gas law that the pressure of the  gas will change  with other gases in the system including  water vapor and also with temperature  many of us are using infrared sensors  these are great for  connecting with monitoring systems for  example  infrared sensors were first introduced  in incubators in 1984  and an infrared sensor is essentially  like  a like a light bulb like a older  incandescent light bulb it has a  tungsten filament  that when electrified gives off light  um and so the gas enters the sensor  it interacts with the light all  wavelengths  except 4.3 micrometers at which  co2 gas absorbs light are  filtered out and then and then that that  amount of co2 gas  triggers an influx of carbon dioxide gas  as needed  now the thing about a tungsten infrared  bulb is  that that tungsten filament degrades  over time as it's heated  and so as a because of that the light  intensity decreases over time as well  when the light intensity decreases that  contributes to decreased  accuracy of the measurement and drift  of that measurement so one of the  options  is to get a newer technology where  instead of using tungsten  a silicon light source is used to  provide that  light and this turns out to be much more  stable  and much more long-lived than tungsten  bulbs it's kind of the equivalent of  moving from an  incandescent light bulb to an led light  bulb  and so so as i said a silicon light  source is much more stable  and long-lived than an older tungsten  style infrared