nt  it's broad spectrum it will kill pretty  much anything  um including the thyroscopic two virus  um but it's it's a little bit difficult  to automate  so there's a lot of handling involved  and um  the problem with an automated system for  hydrogen peroxide is  that there are areas that maybe will be  shadowed  from the mist and if that  if those droplets miss an area of  of the incubator chamber then any  microorganisms there would obviously  survive because they wouldn't be  contacted  by that hydrogen peroxide disinfectant  um so look for proof of efficacy  according to  the united states and european  pharmacopoeias or the pharmacopoeia  relevant for urination  there they've gotten pretty harmonized  in that  instead of for example for heat  sterilization  they used to recommend a given  temperature and time so 160 degrees for  two hours and that would constitute  sterilization  sterilization but it's now recognized  that if you don't reach that given  temperature  um microorganisms may survive because  they're not actually at 160 degrees for  two hours for example  so now the pharmacopoeias recommend or  require  proof of sterilization using one million  organisms resistant bacterial spores  which are proven  to have a specific devalue  and i won't go into what that all means  right now but my point is that  sterilization is now defined as  elimination of one million resistant  microorganisms and those microorganisms  are specified  biological indicators so look for a  process  that has been independently tested  to verify that it meets that  sterilization requirement  and there are companies that will come  in and do a  manual chemical disinfectant  on your incubator and many of those are  good  make sure that it won't corrode or cause  degradation of the electronics in your  incubator  and make sure that that company is going  to verify neutralization of the chemical  that they use  and document the efficacy in the end  that they truly did sterilize your  