ion then that can  play a role here so for most primary  cells i would recommend  a hypoxic incubator um  so if you look at a graph of primary  cell growth  in an incubator that is controlling  oxygen to simulate the in vivo  conditions and one that's not  in the one that's not you might see the  cells grow for a day or two when first  removed from the body  and then as you describe you'll see a  lag  where they'll just they won't  necessarily die but they'll just kind of  be in  in stasis for a little while they're  cellular couch potatoes  and then after you know several more  days you might  see the growth curve start to go up  where they're actually starting to  multiply  what's happening during that lag is that  the cells are actually mutating  genetically to adjust to those  artificial conditions  so i would encourage you to look at  hypoxia and do some research in the  primary literature to see how other  people  are incubating those cells and what  conditions they're  using because that can play a big role  in how primary cells grow especially if  you're looking for them  to mimic conditions in the body you want  them to be  genetically similar to you know the way  they were in the body before you put  them in culture  so i hope that helps well thank you very  much mary kay  and thank you for this great  presentation today do you have any  closing  remarks for our audience before we close  today  um i guess i would just say um to  re-emphasize  um a lot of the information that i  presented today was  unknown to me when i worked in the lab i  kind of assumed that  an incubator is very similar to another  incubator and i hope i've demonstrated  that in  practice that's actually not true and  that that can have effects on your cells  so if you're thinking about a new  incubator purchase especially if you're  in a  cellular gene therapy company  and you're thinking about a large  purchase one thing to do  is to consider buying a single unit for  you to test  to 